<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luigi Pirandello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luigi Pirandello was an Italian dramatist, novelist, poet and short story writer, best known for his plays. Born in Girgenti (now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a>) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to a family that had fought for unification, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1887 to study. He wrote novels, hundreds of short stories and about 40 plays, some in Sicilian; Six Characters in Search of an Author, staged in Rome in 1921, divided its first audience. A supporter of Italian fascism, he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> in 1924 to admit him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> and in 1925 took over the Teatro d'Arte di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1934, and his tragic farces are often seen as forerunners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of the Absurd.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luigi-pirandello.jpg" alt="Luigi Pirandello" width="330" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agence de presse Meurisse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luigi_Pirandello_1932.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
